## 3. Safety rules

### Layering — strictly one-directional
```
app/(site) · app/admin  →  components/  →  lib/  →  content/
```
- `components/` does presentation only and **never** imports from `lib/db/`.
- `content/` holds fixed vocabularies as code constants (taxonomy, nav, site
  details, hero segments). Product data lives in Postgres, never in `content/`.

### Database and admin
- **`await requireAdmin()` is the first statement of every mutating server
  action.** Not after parsing, not after a guard — first. Server actions are
  independently addressable POST endpoints; page-level auth does not protect
  them.
- `app/(site)/actions.ts` is the only unauthenticated write path. Every action
  there carries all three of: a honeypot returning the ordinary success message,
  a `rateLimit()` keyed on the client address, and bounded/validated values.
  A new public action carries all three or belongs in the admin file.
- **Re-validate everything server-side**, including `<select>` values, `min`/`max`
  and hidden fields. HTML attributes are hints to the browser, not controls.
- Adding an editable field is a **seven-file recipe** — follow `docs/ADMIN.md`
  §6 in order. In `lib/db/products.ts` you must update the row type,
  `mapProductRow`, `writeParams` **and both column lists**; the `$n` placeholders
  shift, and that is the step that silently corrupts writes if missed.
- `schema.sql` is applied idempotently on deploy — new columns use
  `ALTER TABLE … ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS`. Never write a destructive migration
  into it.
- Reads go through `safeQuery`, which returns `[]` when the database is absent
  or down. Writes deliberately throw so the admin sees failures. Keep that split.
- Product-facing routes stay **`force-dynamic`**. ISR was tried and rejected:
  `revalidatePath` still serves the stale copy to the next request, so an admin
  who saved a product and reloaded saw the old version.

### Files and uploads
- **Uploads never go in `public/`.** That directory is baked into the Docker
  image and anything written there at runtime vanishes on the next deploy.
  `UPLOAD_DIR` (default `data/uploads`) is a mounted volume, served by
  `/media/[...path]`.
- `/media/[...path]` accepts exactly **one flat segment** and rejects anything
  else. That is the traversal defence — do not "improve" it into a filter.

### Layout and visual constraints
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §9 lists load-bearing constraints; each encodes a real
  bug. Read it before changing sticky offsets, the hero, the footer, the curtain
  sheets or the header.
- Curtain pages (`/`, `/products`, `/about`, `/contact`) mark their sheet with
  **`data-curtain`**; `layout/Header` reads it to be pushed off the top. Removing
  the attribute degrades silently — no error, just wrong behaviour.
- `data-featured-footer` on `FeaturedCard`'s footer is a cross-component contract
  with `home/FeaturedProducts` autoplay pausing. Renaming it silently disables
  that.
- `ProductCard`'s vertical and featured branches size themselves with an
  **invisible clone**. Content added to a card footer must fit the reserved
  height (36px) or every card in the grid changes size.

### Verification before claiming done
- `npx tsc --noEmit` — and if it reports errors in `.next/dev/types/validator.ts`,
  that is stale generated output from a killed dev server: `rm -rf .next/dev/types`
  and re-run.
- `npm run build`.
- `npm run lint` — **there is a known baseline of 15 pre-existing problems
  (10 errors, 5 warnings)**. Compare against that number; do not "fix" unrelated
  pre-existing ones as part of another change.
- Anything visual: run the dev server and drive a real browser. Static reading
  misses rendering bugs. Note the intro splash intercepts pointer events for
  ~2.3s after load.

### Documentation is part of the change
Per `AGENTS.md`: any change that touches structure, adds/removes a dependency,
adds a client component, or alters a §9 constraint **must update
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` in the same change and add a dated change-log entry**.
Content-only edits (`src/content/`, copy, product data) do not need one.

### Git
Do not commit or push unless asked. Do not commit in the server's deploy
directory — the console treats the checked-out SHA as "what is deployed".
